{
  "narrative": "Across diagnostic accuracy studies, large language models (LLMs) frequently achieve sensitivities at or above the 90% threshold, suggesting strong capability to identify relevant records. Li et al. reported average abstract‑screening sensitivity of 90% (range 90–97%) for GPT‑4, with high specificity in some sets but marked variation (59–93%) [R0011]. Oami et al. showed that a modified prompt raised GPT‑4 Turbo sensitivity from 0.75 to 0.91 while maintaining specificity of 0.98, indicating that prompt engineering can markedly improve performance [R0010]. Qiu et al. observed 98–100% sensitivity across three independent datasets, with specificity ranging from 80.6% to 93%, reinforcing the consistency of high sensitivity across domains [R0079]. Zhan et al. reported an even higher sensitivity of 99.2% for title/abstract screening using Review Copilot (GPT‑4), albeit with lower specificity (83.6%) [R0049]. In contrast, López‑Pineda et al. found lower sensitivities for Llama 3 (77.5%) and ChatGPT‑4o mini (56.2%) despite high specificity, highlighting model‑ and configuration‑dependent variability [R0037]. Ramchandani et al. achieved peri‑operative recall of 0.89 but pre‑operative recall dropped to 0.67, showing that task complexity influences sensitivity [R0014]. Overall, studies using dual‑reviewer reference standards and larger sample sizes (e.g., Oami, Li, Qiu) report higher sensitivities, whereas smaller or single‑reviewer designs (e.g., Yamoah) show modestly lower values. Risk‑of‑bias concerns include unblinded reference standards, limited reporting of prompt details, and reliance on single‑reviewer coding, which temper confidence in the pooled estimate.",
  "certainty": "low",
  "certainty_rationale": "Evidence is downgraded for risk of bias (unblinded reference standards, single‑reviewer designs) and inconsistency (wide sensitivity ranges across models and tasks)."
}

{
  "narrative": "Ensemble and prompt‑optimization strategies consistently improve LLM screening accuracy compared with single‑model baselines. In Oami et al., modifying the prompt raised integrated sensitivity from 0.75 to 0.91 while specificity remained above 0.98, demonstrating the impact of prompt engineering on a dataset of 16,670 records [R0010]. Sanghera et al. reported that zero‑shot prompting combined with optimal prompt selection achieved up to 100% sensitivity on a balanced development set and maintained high sensitivity (0.756‑1.000) on a large 119,695‑record cohort, though precision fell markedly due to class imbalance; ensemble LLM‑human combinations restored precision to 0.1450 while preserving perfect sensitivity, highlighting the benefit of model ensembles for workload reduction [R0025]. Guo et al. found that a single GPT API with a criteria‑based prompt yielded overall accuracy of 0.91 and a macro F1 of 0.60 on 24,307 abstracts, with higher inter‑rater agreement (κ=0.96) than between two human reviewers (κ=0.46), suggesting that well‑crafted prompts can approach human consistency [R0028]. Hilkenmeier et al. showed that requiring full agreement across three distinct LLMs produced precision 0.88, recall 0.96, and F1 0.92, outperforming prior single‑LLM results, indicating that consensus ensembles mitigate individual model errors [R0061]. Wang et al. confirmed that calibrated zero‑shot ensembles outperformed individual generative LLMs, emphasizing the role of calibration and ensemble weighting for higher work‑saved‑screening and precision [R0138]. Across studies, higher sensitivity often coincided with lower precision when class imbalance was extreme, and variability in performance appears linked to LLM version, dataset size, and prompt design. Risk of bias was generally rated as some concerns, and several reports lacked full sample details, tempering confidence in the magnitude of gains.",
  "certainty": "moderate",
  "certainty_rationale": "Evidence is observational with some risk of bias and inconsistency across datasets and prompting methods, leading to downgrades for risk of bias and inconsistency."
